# The same fit, which keeps both figures itself. `create_figures` makes two in one body, each kept in fields of the value
# it runs on (`self.fig, self.ax = plt.subplots()`, `self.res_fig, self.res_ax = plt.subplots()`): each binding is a slot
# of the class, so a fit holds two figures (#178), as two calls make two (#175). A class that keeps more than one figure
# names each by the first field its binding sets: `fit.fig` and `fit.res_fig`. Two figures, as on the clean side; what
# differs is what holds them, one value in two slots against two charts in two parts.

route notebooks.residuals
step residuals/draw_fit
step residuals/draw_fit/Fit()
step residuals/draw_fit/Fit.draw
choice residuals/draw_fit/Fit.draw/?1
step residuals/draw_fit/Fit.draw/?1/Fit.create_figures

line entry > residuals call
line residuals/draw_fit/Fit() > Fit·fit@residuals/draw_fit/Fit() value
line Fit·fit@residuals/draw_fit/Fit() > residuals/draw_fit/Fit.draw/?1/Fit.create_figures value
line residuals/draw_fit/Fit.draw/?1/Fit.create_figures > Fit·self@residuals/draw_fit/Fit.draw/?1 value
line residuals/draw_fit/Fit.draw/?1/Fit.create_figures > Figure 1 · Fit · fit.fig own        # `self.fig, self.ax = plt.subplots()`
line residuals/draw_fit/Fit.draw/?1/Fit.create_figures > Figure 2 · Fit · fit.res_fig own    # `self.res_fig, self.res_ax = plt.subplots()`
line Fit·self@residuals/draw_fit/Fit.draw/?1 > residuals/draw_fit/Fit.draw value   # `self.ax.plot(..)`, `self.res_ax.plot(..)`
line residuals/draw_fit/Fit.draw > Fit·fit@residuals/draw_fit/Fit.draw value
line Fit·fit@residuals/draw_fit/Fit.draw > residuals/draw_fit value           # `return fit`
line residuals/draw_fit > Fit·fit@residuals/draw_fit value                     # `fit = draw_fit(..)`

home Fit·fit@residuals/draw_fit/Fit() residuals/draw_fit
home Fit·self@residuals/draw_fit/Fit.draw/?1 residuals/draw_fit/Fit.draw
home Fit·fit@residuals/draw_fit/Fit.draw residuals/draw_fit
home Fit·fit@residuals/draw_fit residuals

side entry left
side Figure 1 · Fit · fit.fig right
